# How much would a planner need to know to set the right tax?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What information does an optimal second-best tax demand? How badly can a small error go wrong?"
next: {"title":"Why do governments fail even when they mean well?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/55-why-do-governments-fail-even-when-they.cast"}

## Where did the soda go?
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - before
      - city stores
      - city + nearby
    values:
      - 100
      - 54
      - 78
    value_labels: true
    colors:
      - grey
      - red
      - orange
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 110
    y_label: sweetened drinks sold (% of pre-tax)
    gap: 0.45
    label_size: 30
    box:
      x: 60
      'y': 150
      w: 600
      h: 480
    x_label: a year after the tax
sources:
    - id: seiler2021
      title: 'The Impact of Soda Taxes: Pass-Through, Tax Avoidance, and Nutritional Effects'
      authors: Stephan Seiler, Anna Tuchman, Song Yao
      year: 2021
      finding: Philadelphia's 1.5 cents-per-ounce tax on sweetened beverages (from 1 January 2017) cut taxed-beverage volume in city stores by 46%; increased purchases in stores outside the city offset more than half of that, so the net reduction was 22% (retail scanner data).
      url: https://papers.ssrn.com/abstract=3302335

    text cue_tax "Philadelphia\n1.5¢ per ounce\nfrom 1 Jan 2017" #b5482e x 840 y 520 font_size 34 hidden true
    text cue_leak "the leak:\nmore than half\nof the lost sales\njust moved" #d0865f cites ["seiler2021"] x 840 y 470 font_size 32 hidden true
    text cue_who "Seiler, Tuchman & Yao\nscanner data" #8a5fa8 cites ["seiler2021"] x 840 y 290 font_size 26 hidden true
    text cue_need "the right tax needs:\nthe harm\n+ where buyers go" x 840 y 260 font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "Where did the soda go?"

How much would a planner need to know to set the right tax? Take Philadelphia, which in 2017 put a tax on sweetened drinks.
    draw axes bar_1

B: One and a half cents an ounce. On a two-litre bottle, about a dollar.
    draw cue_tax

Before the tax, call the city's sales a hundred.
    highlight bar_1

    ask "Drag the city-stores bar to your guess." on bar_2 store g default "80" voice b
        intro: Guess first: a year on, how much did the city's own stores sell?
        right: Close: you said {g}; it was {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g}; it was {g.true}.

    erase cue_tax

Fifty-four. Sales in Philadelphia's stores fell by forty-six percent.
    highlight bar_2 effect box

B: So people drank about half as much soda?
    highlight bar_2

Not quite. Count the stores ju (@draw bar_3@)st outside the city line too, and the drop is only twenty-two percent.

B: Because people drove a few miles and loaded up the car where there was no tax.
    highlight bar_3 effect box

More than half of the lost sales simply moved.
    draw cue_leak

The economists Stephan Seiler, Anna Tuchman and Song Yao found it in the stores' own scanner data.
    draw cue_who

    erase cue_who

A tax on a harm needs to know the harm; that much Pigou's rule already says (@draw cue_need@). This one also needs to know where the buyers go.

B: And nobody knew the size of that leak until after the tax was in.
    highlight cue_leak

    pause 0.4

## A hill with a flat top
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 50
    'y':
      - -130
      - 220
    box:
      x: 80
      'y': 120
      w: 540
      h: 520
vars:
    h:
      value: 40
      min: 20
      max: 60
    d:
      value: 0.5
      min: 0.2
      max: 0.95
    T:
      expr: h*(1-d)
    Gm:
      expr: 0.25*h*(1-d)*h*(1-d)

    text town "Harbourtown\n(illustrative)" #8f887c x 820 y 610 font_size 30 hidden true
    icon bottle size 60 x 700 y 530 icon_look picture of bottle hidden true
    text harm "harm h = 40¢ a bottle" x 840 y 530 font_size 30 hidden true
    text noleak "no leak: tax = h" #8f887c x 820 y 465 font_size 30 hidden true
    icon ferry size 60 x 700 y 460 icon_look picture of ferry hidden true
    text leak "mainland: no tax" #8f887c x 845 y 455 font_size 28 hidden true
    text dlab "leak d = {d:2}" #d0865f x 845 y 410 font_size 30 hidden true
    math eq tex "t^* = h \\times \\blank{(1-d)}" size 34 x 820 y 360 hidden true
    math eq2 tex "t^* = h \\times (1-d)" size 34 x 820 y 360 hidden true
    text eqv "t* = 40 × (1 − {d:2}) = {T:0}¢" #2f6b8f x 815 y 295 font_size 28 hidden true
    axes ax y_label "town's gain ($ a week)" ticks.x [0,10,20,30,40,50] ticks.y [-100,0,100,200] hidden true
    text xlab "tax per bottle (¢) →" at.data [24,-122] font_size 26 hidden true
    curve hill #2f6b8f expr "0.5*(x*h*(1-d) - x*x/2)" x_from 0 x_to 50 hidden true
    dot hilltop #2f6b8f at.on hill at.x 20 bind {"at.x":"h*(1-d)"} hidden true
    label lab_top "{T:0}¢: ${Gm:0} a week" above #2f6b8f attach_to hilltop hidden true
    dot p15 #8f887c at.on hill at.x 15 hidden true
    dot p25 #8f887c at.on hill at.x 25 hidden true
    label lab15 "15¢: $94" below-left #8f887c attach_to p15 hidden true
    label lab25 "25¢: $94" below-right #8f887c attach_to p25 hidden true
    curve neg #b5482e expr "0.5*(x*h*(1-d) - x*x/2)" x_from 40 x_to 50 bind.x_from 2*h*(1-d) hidden true
    text lab_neg "worse than\nnothing" #b5482e at.data [44,45] font_size 28 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch sc center work [{"tex":"\\text{best gain: } G(T) = \\tfrac{1}{2}\\,b\\,T^2"},{"tex":"\\text{miss by } e:\\ \\text{lose } \\tfrac{1}{2}\\,b\\,e^2"},{"tex":"\\text{share lost} = (e/T)^2"},{"tex":"e = 5,\\ T = 20:\\ \\text{about } 6\\%"},{"tex":"e = 5,\\ T = 8:\\ \\text{about } 39\\%"}] font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "A hill with a flat top"

B: That leak is the whole story. Let's shrink it to Harbourtown, with illustrative numbers.
    draw town ax xlab parallel true

B: The council taxes sugary drinks in the town's shops. Say ea (@draw bottle harm@)ch bottle drunk costs the town forty cents in clinic bills.

With no leak, the rule is easy: tax the harm, forty cents.
    draw noleak

    erase noleak

B: But some buyers take the ferry and buy on the mainland. Those bottles are still drunk, still do the harm, and pay no tax.
    draw ferry leak

B: Call d the share of the lost sale (@draw dlab@)s that leaks. Say a half. Every bottle that leaks was taxed away for nothing.

    draw eq

    ask "Which tile finishes the right tax?" on eq others ["d","(1+d)"] store f
        right: One minus d: only the share that stays home is harm avoided.
        wrong: It's one minus d: only the share that doesn't leak is harm avoided.

So the right tax is twenty cents.
    draw eqv

    erase eq eq_tiles_1 eq_tiles_2 eq_tiles_3

    draw eq2

Now plot what the town gains at every tax, in dollars a week.
    highlight ax

A hill, the same shape any second-best fix draws.
    draw hill

Its top is at twenty cents: a hundred dollars a week.
    draw hilltop lab_top

B: And look how flat the top is. Miss by five cen (@draw p15 p25 lab15 lab25@)ts either way and you still keep ninety-four.

That's the reassuring half: near the best tax, a small error costs little.
    highlight hilltop p15 p25

    erase p15 p25 lab15 lab25

B: But past forty cents, twice the  (@draw neg lab_neg@)best tax, the hill drops below zero. There the tax does more harm than good.

    erase lab_neg

And if more leaks, the hill shrinks and its top slides left.
    animate d 0.75 duration 3 ghost true

The best tax is whatever harm is left after the leak.
    highlight hilltop lab_top

    animate d 0.5 duration 1.5

    draw lab_neg

    erase hill_ghost

    fade ax xlab hill hilltop lab_top neg lab_neg town bottle harm ferry leak eq2 eqv dlab to 0.15

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want to see where the hill comes from?

@math

    erase mathq

Here's what's new. At its top the town gains a half times b times T squared, T being the harm left after the leak.
    draw sc_box sc_line_1

Miss the top by e cents and you lose a half b e squared, whatever T is.
    draw sc_line_2

So the share of the gain you lose is e over T, squared.
    draw sc_line_3

With T at twenty, a five-cent miss costs about six percent of the gain.
    draw sc_line_4

With T at eight, the same miss costs about forty.
    draw sc_line_5

    erase sc

@test

    erase mathq

    fade ax xlab hill hilltop lab_top neg lab_neg town bottle harm ferry leak eq2 eqv dlab to 1

## When the leak is guessed
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 50
    'y':
      - -130
      - 220
    box:
      x: 80
      'y': 120
      w: 540
      h: 520
vars:
    h:
      value: 40
      min: 30
      max: 50
    d:
      value: 0.5
      min: 0.3
      max: 0.9
    T:
      expr: h*(1-d)
    G:
      expr: 0.5*(20*h*(1-d) - 200)
sources:
    - id: roberto2019
      title: Association of a Beverage Tax on Sugar-Sweetened and Artificially Sweetened Beverages With Changes in Beverage Prices and Sales at Chain Retailers in a Large Urban Setting
      authors: Christina A. Roberto, Hannah G. Lawman, Michael T. LeVasseur, et al.
      year: 2019
      finding: Philadelphia's taxed-beverage volume at chain retailers fell 51%; higher sales in bordering areas offset about a quarter (24.4%) of that, a net reduction of 38%. JAMA 321(18):1799–1810.
      doi: 10.1001/jama.2019.4249
    - id: seiler2021
      title: 'The Impact of Soda Taxes: Pass-Through, Tax Avoidance, and Nutritional Effects'
      authors: Stephan Seiler, Anna Tuchman, Song Yao
      year: 2021
      finding: City-store volume fell 46%; cross-shopping outside the city offset more than half of it, net 22%.
      url: https://papers.ssrn.com/abstract=3302335

    axes ax y_label "town's gain ($ a week)" ticks.x [0,10,30,40,50] ticks.y [-100,0,100,200] hidden true
    text xlab "tax per bottle (¢) →" at.data [16,-122] font_size 26 hidden true
    curve believed dashed #8f887c expr "0.5*(x*20 - x*x/2)" x_from 0 x_to 50 hidden true
    curve hill #2f6b8f expr "0.5*(x*h*(1-d) - x*x/2)" x_from 0 x_to 50 hidden true
    curve plan dashed #8f887c x_expr "20" y_expr t t_from -105 t_to 190 hidden true
    text plan_lab "her tax: 20¢" #8f887c at.data [20,205] font_size 26 hidden true
    dot herpt #b5482e at.on hill at.x 20 hidden true
    text planner "planner guesses d = ½\n→ sets 20¢" #8f887c x 820 y 590 font_size 28 hidden true
    text dlab "true leak d = {d:2}" #d0865f x 820 y 500 font_size 32 hidden true
    text hlab "harm h = {h:0}¢" x 820 y 455 font_size 30 hidden true
    text tlab "best tax {T:0}¢" #2f6b8f x 820 y 410 font_size 30 hidden true
    text glab "her gain: ${G:0} a week" #b5482e x 820 y 340 font_size 34 hidden true
    text worse "worse than\nno tax" #b5482e at.data [37,-85] font_size 30 hidden true
    text leftover "1 − d: ½ → ⅕\nbest tax −60%" #b5482e x 820 y 230 font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "When the leak is guessed"

A flat top, then, but only if you know where the top is. And the planner has to guess the leak before the tax starts.
    draw ax xlab believed

B: Say she gue (@draw planner plan plan_lab@)sses a half, like us, and sets twenty cents.

That's the hill she believes in.
    highlight believed

The real one depends on the true leak.
    draw hill herpt dlab hlab tlab glab

B: So try four-fifths. Philadelphia's two big studies put its leak at about a quarter and at more than a half: a gap that size is no wild miss.
    highlight dlab

My hunch would be: a smaller gain, but still a gain.
    highlight herpt

    quiz "If the true leak is four-fifths, what does her 20¢ tax do?" judge false voice b pool false
        right: Watch her point as the true hill moves.
        * Still a gain, just smaller
        * About nothing either way
        * Worse than no tax

The hill shrinks to a top at eight cents, and her twenty cents lands at minus twenty dollars a week.
    animate d 0.8 duration 3

Worse than no tax at all.
    draw worse

    erase worse

    animate d 0.5 duration 1.5

B: And if she'd got the harm wrong instead? Say thirty-two cents, not forty.
    highlight hlab

Then she still keeps sixty dollars out of a possible sixty-four. A twenty percent error in the harm barely matters.
    animate h 32 duration 2.5

B: Why is the leak so much worse?
    animate h 40 duration 1.5

Because the tax is built on what's left after the leak: one (@draw leftover@) minus d. A half shrinking to a fifth cuts the best tax by sixty percent.
    animate d 0.8 duration 2.5

Your turn: drag the true leak between three-tenths and four-fifths, and watch her gain swing from a hundred and eighty dollars to a loss.
    explore params ["d"]

    erase leftover

## Know more, or need less?
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: A planner who knows where her leaks are.
    good: Most of the leaks found.
    poor: Some bottles slipped across the border.
    none: Even Philadelphia needed two studies.
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 50
    'y':
      - -130
      - 430
    box:
      x: 80
      'y': 130
      w: 540
      h: 510
vars:
    h:
      value: 40
      fixed: true
    d:
      value: 0.8
      min: 0
      max: 0.9
    T:
      expr: h*(1-d)
    Gm:
      expr: 0.25*h*(1-d)*h*(1-d)
sources:
    - id: chetty2009
      title: 'Sufficient Statistics for Welfare Analysis: A Bridge Between Structural and Reduced-Form Methods'
      authors: Raj Chetty
      year: 2009
      finding: Welfare effects of many policies can be expressed in terms of a few high-level elasticities (sufficient statistics) that can be estimated without a full structural model. Annual Review of Economics 1:451–488.
    - id: roberto2019
      title: Association of a Beverage Tax on Sugar-Sweetened and Artificially Sweetened Beverages With Changes in Beverage Prices and Sales at Chain Retailers in a Large Urban Setting
      authors: Christina A. Roberto et al.
      year: 2019
      finding: Border-area sales offset about a quarter (24.4%) of Philadelphia's 51% drop; net 38%.
      doi: 10.1001/jama.2019.4249
    - id: seiler2021
      title: 'The Impact of Soda Taxes: Pass-Through, Tax Avoidance, and Nutritional Effects'
      authors: Stephan Seiler, Anna Tuchman, Song Yao
      year: 2021
      finding: Cross-shopping offset more than half of the 46% in-city drop; net 22%. A wider tax area would limit the avoidance.
      url: https://papers.ssrn.com/abstract=3302335

    axes ax y_label "town's gain ($ a week)" ticks.x [0,10,20,30,40,50] ticks.y [0,200,400] hidden true
    text xlab "tax per bottle (¢) →" at.data [16,-110] font_size 26 hidden true
    curve hill #2f6b8f expr "0.5*(x*h*(1-d) - x*x/2)" x_from 0 x_to 50 hidden true
    dot hilltop #2f6b8f at.on hill at.x 8 bind {"at.x":"h*(1-d)"} hidden true
    label lab_top "best {T:0}¢: ${Gm:0} a week" above #2f6b8f attach_to hilltop hidden true
    dot zero #b5482e at.data [0,0] hidden true
    text zero_lab "no tax =\na guess too" #b5482e at.data [10,340] font_size 32 hidden true
    text dlab "leak d = {d:2}" #d0865f x 820 y 470 font_size 32 hidden true
    text close "tax the mainland too\n→ no leak: t* = h" #2f6b8f x 820 y 380 font_size 30 hidden true
    text trap "one channel untaxed\n→ the tax needs the crossings" #8a5fa8 x 820 y 270 font_size 30 hidden true
    text side_a "a few well-measured\nresponses are enough" #2f6b8f cites ["chetty2009"] x 270 y 520 font_size 34 hidden true
    text side_a2 "Raj Chetty, 2009" #8f887c x 270 y 430 font_size 28 hidden true
    text side_b "those responses are what\ncareful studies disagree on" #b5482e x 730 y 520 font_size 34 hidden true
    text side_b2 "Philadelphia's leak:\nabout ¼? more than ½?" #8f887c cites ["roberto2019","seiler2021"] x 730 y 410 font_size 28 hidden true
    text stamp1 "the tax needs harm + responses + leaks: CONSENSUS" #2f6b8f x 500 y 280 font_size 30 hidden true
    text stamp2 "measured well enough to beat a simple rule? CONTESTED" #b5482e x 500 y 215 font_size 30 hidden true
    text final "the less that's left\nafter the leaks,\nthe bigger a small error\nnext to the gain" x 820 y 440 font_size 32 hidden true
    arrow zero_arr #b5482e from.data [5,290] to.data [0.8,20] hidden true

    card title "Know more, or need less?"

B: So a planner who isn't sure should leave it alone?
    draw ax xlab hill hilltop dlab

That's a guess as well. No tax is a tax of zero,
    draw zero zero_lab zero_arr

and if the leak is small, zero (@draw lab_top@) throws away most of the hill.
    animate d 0.3 duration 2.5

B: Then shrink the guessing. Tax the whole region, and the leak closes.
    draw close

With nothing leaking, the hill grows back to Pigou's, and the best tax is just the harm. One hard number instead of several.
    animate d 0 duration 3

B: That's the second-best trap in miniature: leave one channel untaxed, and the right tax on the others depends on how buyers cross between them.
    draw trap

    erase ax xlab hill hilltop lab_top zero zero_lab dlab close trap zero_arr

Some economists, following Raj Chetty, argue that a few well-measured responses are enough to set policy, without the whole model.
    draw side_a side_a2

B: And others reply that those responses are exactly what careful studies disagree on. Philadelphia's leak: a quarter, or more than half?
    draw side_b side_b2

How sure are we? That the best t (@draw stamp1@)ax needs the harm plus how buyers respond and where they go is consensus.

Whether we can  (@draw stamp2@)measure them well enough to beat a simple rule is contested.

    animate d 0.8 duration 0.1

    erase side_a side_a2 side_b side_b2 stamp1 stamp2

Three new cases.
    draw ax xlab hill hilltop lab_top

    quiz "A city taxes plastic carrier bags, and shoppers buy more plastic bin liners instead. Compared with the harm of a bag, the best bag tax is…"
        right: Lower: some of the plastic the tax stops comes back as bin liners, so less harm is avoided per bag.
        wrong: Some of the plastic the tax stops comes back in another bag, so less harm is avoided per bag: here, lower.
        + lower
        * the same
        * higher

    quiz "A planner sets a tax of 30. The best tax turns out to be 18. Is her tax still better than no tax?" voice b
        right: Yes: the gain only turns negative past twice the best tax, 36, so 30 still helps, just less.
        wrong: Think about where the hill crosses zero: twice the best tax, 36. Here, yes, 30 is under 36.
        + Yes
        * No

    quiz "A region taxes petrol, but drivers fill up across the border. What most cuts how much the planner must know?"
        right: Taxing the neighbour too: close the leak and the best tax falls back to the harm alone.
        wrong: Which number made her guess so risky? The leak. Here, tax the neighbouring region too.
        + Tax petrol in the neighbouring region too
        * Measure the harm of petrol more precisely
        * Double the tax

So how much would a planner need to know? Not just the harm, but every way around the tax and how many take it.
    highlight hill hilltop

The less that's l (@draw final@)eft after the leaks, the bigger a small error is next to what the tax can gain, and whether any state can know enough is where the argument goes next.

    pause 0.5

## Where next
end_page: true

    text end_next_kicker "Next" x 500 y 540 font_size 26 style.opacity 0.6
    link end_next href lecture:55 title "Why do governments fail even when they mean well?" form title size 600 x 500 y 420

    link end_again href lecture:54 title "Watch again" form text mark replay open here x 500 y 250 font_size 28
    text end_count "54 of 365" x 500 y 180 font_size 22 style.opacity 0.6
